Installation

For official CRS plugin installation instructions, see https://coreruleset.org/docs/concepts/plugins/#how-to-install-a-plugin.

Which archive to download
=========================

Releases after 1.2.0 ship plugin-only archives that contain the plugins/
directory and documentation, and nothing else:

  wordpress-hardening-plugin-<version>.tar.gz
  wordpress-hardening-plugin-<version>.zip
  SHA256SUMS

Use these with automated CRS plugin installers (CRS itself, BunkerWeb, ...).
Such installers search the extracted tree recursively for *-config.conf and
reject any match that does not declare a plugin name and version, so the CI
test harness must not be present.

For those same releases the GitHub-generated "Source code" archives are
equivalent, because the test and CI trees are marked export-ignore. That
attribute is read from the commit being archived, so it does not apply
retroactively: the "Source code" archives of 1.2.0 and earlier still contain
tests/ and fail those installers. On 1.2.0 or earlier, either download a
later release or delete the tests/ directory after extracting.

File Path Configuration
=====================

This plugin uses @pmFromFile / @ipMatchFromFile for efficient pattern matching.
The following .data files must all be deployed alongside the .conf files (or
referenced with an absolute path):

  - wordpress-hardening-files.data            (direct-access block list)
  - wordpress-hardening-scanners.data         (scanner UA block list, PL2)
  - wordpress-hardening-login-countries.data  (GeoIP allow list — LOWERCASE)
  - wordpress-hardening-ip-reputation.data    (IP reputation block list)
  - wordpress-hardening-trusted-proxies.data  (XFF trusted-proxy CIDRs)

The files must be in one of:

1. The same directory as the .conf file that references them (keep the plugin
   files together), OR
2. ModSecurity's configured rules directory, OR
3. Referenced with an absolute path in the rules file:
     @pmFromFile /full/path/to/wordpress-hardening-files.data

Optional IP rules (all three engines)
====================================

plugins/wordpress-hardening-ip.conf is NOT loaded by the standard CRS plugin
loader. Include it explicitly to retain client-IP/XFF resolution, trusted-proxy
pinning, private-client exemptions and IP reputation. Keep the two IP .data
files alongside it. Without this include, the base rules still protect endpoints
and do not exempt private clients.

Order: CRS setup, plugin *-config.conf and operator overrides, optional IP file,
plugin *-before.conf, optional rate-limit file, CRS rules, plugin *-after.conf.
With an automatic loader, insert the explicit IP include between its config and
before stages; do not include the same file twice. Set operator overrides,
including wordpress-hardening-plugin_enabled=0, before the IP file.

GeoIP stays in the base rules; only its private-client exemption needs the IP
include. IP reputation also needs ip_reputation_enabled=1 and a populated list.
Keep trusted_proxies_enabled=1 and list only proxies that overwrite incoming XFF.

Optional login rate limiting (Apache/mod_security2 only)
=========================================================

plugins/wordpress-hardening-ratelimit.conf is NOT loaded by the standard CRS
plugin loader. It requires persistent collections (load-fatal on Coraza,
unreliable on libmodsecurity3) — include it explicitly on Apache only, after
the plugin before-file. It also requires wordpress-hardening-ip.conf before
the main before-file for its client identity. See README "IP-Based Rate Limiting".

See [README](README.md) for complete installation documentation.
